In Switzerland, dexamethasone (Decadron®) is available in 2 mg, 4 mg (preferentially paediatric form also in drop formulation), and 8 mg tablets (plastics or coated). In solution for injection (Decadron® Injectable 4mg/ml or 10mg/ml), it is available in 2ml vials containing 8 mg or 20 mg (for parenteral use).
A dose of 20 mg is therefore available in the parenteral formulation used for short IV / IM treatments at the CHUV (and other hospitals) among others for acute manometric elevation of intracranial pressure, severe allergic reactions (anaphylactic shock), acute violent asthma where there is significant distress or deterioration, status epilepticus, or in certain endocrine emergencies (e.g. severe adrenal insufficiency). ^{1,2} It cannot, however, be prescribed as an oral treatment.
The Swiss Agency for Therapeutic Products (Swissmedic) issued a customs document on 13 October 2014 regarding dexamethasone tablets qua 'osmotic controlled release', but only in strengths of 0.75 mg and 2 mg for long-term treatment and 1 mg, 3 mg, 6 mg and 8 mg as controlled release tablets for once daily evening intake. ^{3} Oral dexamethasone tablets in dosages over 8 mg would not provide the slow release characteristic of these preparations and therefore do not qualify for registration in Switzerland.
The Swiss Medical Federation recommends prescribing oral doses below 8 mg for patients who can tolerate tablets and swallowing without undue trouble, but suggests that parenteral administration (dexamethasone 8mg i.v./i.m. at night for example) or use of the 4 mg drop formulation, if well tolerated, is preferred over 8mg tablets in for the following cases where rapid preabsorption is not advantageous: in the acute phases of asthma for morning dosing, for morning administration in addition to short courses of inhaled corticosteroids and short acting β 2 agonists (e.g. in an asthma flare leading to nocturnal symptoms), or where gastro-intestinal intolerance precludes tablet intake. ^{4}
Example of correct prescription in Switzerland for a patient you would have ordered to take 20 mg dexamethasone (e.g. for acute manometric elevation of intracranial pressure or severe asthma): 'For the next [insert time] administer 2 x decadron® injectable 10mg/mL iv single dose [if oral intake from the patient is impossible] or continue dexamethasone 8 mg tablets orally in the evening for the next [insert time]'. For example 'for the next 4 days, administer dexamethasone 8 mg tablet orally in the evening, or if not possible: continue 2 x Decadron injectable 10mg/mL IV (i.e. a single dose of 20 mg)'. Written agreement and a follow-up consultation are necessary for initial oral doses of corticosteroids above 8 mg or equivalent parenteral treatment'.
In summary, you should not request the pharmacy to prepare dexamethasone tablets in a strength of 20 mg from 0.75 mg or 2 mg tablets (which to provide this would be adulterating) or compounded from 8 mg tablets. You should either (a) prescribe use of Decadron injectable 20mg (i.e. via IV/IM), or (b) if the patient can tolerate tablets, a dexamethasone prescription for e.g. 'dexamethasone 8mg tablet once per day in the evening for the next [x] days...' (supplemented by an accompanying note). In case of doubt, calling the hospital or local community pharmacy's TBD service or talking to your hospital pharmacy (eg in the case of the CHUV, contact the 'TélService Phamaceutique Général [SPG] CHUV: Tél. + 41 21 3147 625 or by email service.pharmaceutique@chuv.ch, weekdays, 08h-12h 14h-16h), is always a good option. ^{5,6}

