According to the current Swiss Society of Intensive Care Medicine (SSICM/Société Suisse de Réanimation/SWR) recommendations published in the 'Guidelines for the treatment of acute hypoxemic respiratory failure in adults' ( CHEST disorders, DBG, SLTT, SSM, SSV, SSICM/SWR 2016, ^{1} revised in 2021[2]) and Pers Lancet Respir Med 2021 guidelines of the European Society for Intensive Care Medicine (ESICM) et the Swiss Society for Intensive Care Medicine acknowledged as having a national relevance by the Swiss medical society,[3] and which are applicable to this patient with severe pneumonia (by day 3 or the first three hospital days, PaO2/FiO2 ratio ≤ 400 mmHg and/or SpO2 with supplemental oxygen <96%, bilateral infiltrates consistent with pulmonary edema or consolidation on frontal chest X-ray[1] or CT in accord with SpO2 <96% on 4-6 L/min supplemental oxygen by nasal prongs or face mask see appendix 22))) the removal of non-invasive supplemental oxygen in case of a SpO2 ≥ 92%, as is the case here, is recommended. ^{1,2}
Oxygen should be discontinued or reduced in a stepwise manner without pre-oxygenation, from an initial 3 to 1 L/min nasal prongs or 2-4 L/min face mask, aiming for the lowest flow rate allowing a SpO2 ≥ 92% without worsening of respiratory rate and/or signs of new or increased distress (see, for example, patient's respiratory rate <30 and no accessory muscle use). The patient does not require increase in the FiO2.
Re-oxygenation should only be achieved upon a decrease of SpO2 to 90-92% or a decrease in their level of consciousness (Glasgow Coma Score ≤13), as a SpO2 ≥ 92% on room air is normal in healthy black subjects. ^{1, 2, 3}
Oxygen is not inserted or increased in an attempt to achieve an SpO2 target saturations above 96%. Furthermore, it is considered that routine measurements of PaO2 and ABG in order to guide oxygen therapy are not recommended in hypoxemic patients with a SpO2 ≥92% on ≤4 L/min supplemental oxygen by nasal prongs or face mask. ^{1, 2, 3}
Thus for this patient with a stable SpO2 of 92% on supplemental oxygen, no increase of the FiO2 is needed. Oxygen can be discontinued or the supplemental oxygen reduced to 2-4 L/min without a mask during the next 1-2 hours by nursing staff (initial instruction to the nursing staff is usually sufficient – no need to contact the resident or intensivist unless Sp O2 falls to 90-92%). Clinical monitoring of the patient by the nurse is recommended (observation of the patient's respiratory rate, accessory respiratory muscle use, level of consciousness and work of breathing), repeat lung monitoring by X-ray (when symptoms or signs of decompensation occur, eg if respiratory rate increases, use of accessory respiratory muscles, or worsening of the ARDS by an increase in groundglass opacification) or if the SpO2 falls to <90-92%. ^{1, 2}
Example : "Please discontinue supplemental oxygen, monitor the patient's respiratory rate and behaviour during the next 1-2 hours and page me if he shows signs of deterioration (fall of SpO2 to <90%, respiratory rate >30 breaths/min, accessory muscle use, talking in a few short sentences or confusion GCS ≤13)".
The patient should not go to the ICU for the sole reason of an SpO2 of 92% with a FiO2 of 0.24 on face mask (thus no 'obligation ICU transfer' like in the situation of an SpO2 <90%). ^{2}
Please note that if the symptoms of the pneumonia are outside of the early phase (day 3 or first three hospital days) with septic shock, need for mechanical ventilation, or myocardial or multi-organ failure, the situation will be an exception and an oxygen saturation target above 92% may be stated by the treating physician, as part of an orientated initial treatment having as much priority as assessment and fluid administration and starting antibiotics according to current guidelines. ^{1}
Example of the bottom line note on the patients chart: 'Sp O2 92% on 4L/min nasal cannula. No need to increase FiO2. Will be reduced by nursing staff during the next 1-2 hours followed by lung monitoring by chest X-ray if any clinical deterioration (SpO2 to 90-92%, or new respiratory distress)'.
This 'liberal' approach to oxygenation in patients with a SpO2 ≥92% has been shown to reduce the incidence of acute respiratory distress syndrome in severe pneumonia. ^{4, 5, 6} It is in line with both Swiss and the ESICM/SSICM 2021 recommendations for the patient's category.[3] As correct de-escalation of oxygen is linked to a better patient outcome, intensivist expertise is not necessary and the patient does not need to be looked at by an intensivist in this case.
(Calling a 'black Swiss TPD [ SWIFT]-qualified' specialist, often not readily available when the service is quiet in a Monday morning, is not indicated for this reason, in contrast with a severe hypoxemic respiratory failure (SpO2 ≤90% despite 4-6 L/min faces mask and/or need of more oxygen, see appendix 22 of ref 1).
Notably, maintaining a patient on O2 >4L/min in humidified airflow without clinical need is considered incurrent morbidity with, for healthy black patients, no clear mortality benefit [4, 7]. Reducing or discontinuing supplemental oxygen at this stage in Switzerland is even more relevant as «short-term oxygen therapy » for general practices and departments (other than ICU and Intermediate Care in protracted hypoxemic/respiratory failure or septic shock) is not a recommended option in Spain or UK during the first three hospital days of severe pneumonia [8 or 9] (not recommended in case of SpO2/FiO2 ratio ≤400 mmHg on day 1 and a SpO2 on room air ≥95% in black patients whereas these recommendations recommend removal of supplemental oxygen below a SpO2 of 92%, typically at 5-6L/min by face mask).
Example: if the patient develops a significant increase of distress or compromise (increase in respiratory rate to >30/min or drop in SpO2 to <90%) after discontinuation or reduction of FiO2 filtration or noseprongs, analyse respiratory findings to reserve mechanical ventilation, high flow nasal oxygen (usually not needed in Switzerland in this scenario as it requires specific hospital authorisation not requested in these circumstances, unless there is also need for nasal prongs >6L/min which allows its prescription in the wards) and/or CPAP for a short time until a drop in SpO2 back to ≥92%. This will in no case undo the initial non-invasive empirical oxygen de-escalation made according to recommendations.
Quality control is also facilitated by the fact that the ICU in Switzerland is notified only above the threshold for standardised severe hypoxemic and septic shock following appendix 22 of ref 1 [as most recently modified including multitrauma patients and patients not in coma as SSR.../SWISstransfusion network/SSIM recommendations (ref 1 appendix22）。For all other cases of severe pneumonia (type and severity classifying, not including an SpO2 <90%), in particular in patients in the favourable prognosis group with severe pneumonia with an SpO2 ≥92% (i.e., PaO2/FiO2 >400 mmHg AND SpO2 ≥ 96% at day 1 or during days 2-4 and any PaO2/FiO2 ≥300 AND SpO2 ≥92 at day 3-4 in uncomplicated pneumonia), the SSM [14] confirms the 2021 recommendations published by SSR.../SWISstransfusion network/SSIM [1].
Example of physician advice to the nursing staff in the case presented:
'Dureka Francis, please reduce the oxygen flow to 3-2L/min as nasal cannula without a face mask and advise me if there is a new aggravation of the respiratory condition.'

 nghi The possible diagnoses are:
1. Bowel obstruction and ischaemic intussusception withheld perforation
2. Spontaneously reperfused perforation of the intestine (free intraperitoneal fluid and free air, 48 hours duration of sepsis/till perforation) ^{1}
3. Non-ischaemic intussusception (bowel obstruction and abdominal tenderness in a HIV uninfected infant presenting with severe condition but without free air or absent Doppler flow on ultrasound). ^{2}
Immediate surgical consultation or transfer to the operating theatre is required from ED as the child is showing signs of peritoneal sepsis (lethargy, fever >38°C, significant abdominal guarding in the presence of abdominal distension) indicative of impending or established bowel perforation, and not just non-urgent reduced intestinal obstruction due to non-ischaemic intussusception. ^{3} The child would therefore not be suitability assessed or consent obtained in the ED for non-urgent "reduction" by ultrasound guided pneumatic or hydrostatic reduction under sedation or by gastroduodenoscopy which is standard practice for acute, uncomplicated 'idiopathic' intussusception presenting within 24 hours in 4-12 month infants (the RAMIRRO guidelines for Tanzania (2017) and WHO/UNICEF recommendations (2019): <24 hours, abdominal X-ray not showing free air, peristalsis present on the X-ray or on POCUS/no free fluid and Doppler flow present in intussusceptum in ileocolic intussusception, core temperature <38°C and no systemic signs of peritonitis). ^{2,4}
In this case immediate action should be taken as for anything less than 24 hours' duration presenting with systemic signs of peritonitis, marked abdominal distension with guarding, fever for >48hours, free intraperitoneal fluid or free air: such an infant requires an urgent surgical intervention which is outside the experience and facilities of most hospital EDs in Tanzania, including Muhimbili. ^{3} In Dar es Salaam, where major intestinal surgery is readily available, the infant should be immediately referred to the gastrointestinal surgical team without delay in the ED, ideally by phone contact made by the resident on duty (urgent referral label or requisition are neither necessary nor time-saving).
Marked hypoglycaemia (glucose 3.4mmol/L) requires early, ongoing intravenous fluid resuscitation with normal saline (target 80-100ml/kg in first hour) _{.l} Glucose should be given when serum glucose drops to <2.5mmol/L (in particular 10ml/kg 10% glucose IV if 1 litre of fluid where Na 130-134mmol/L or normal saline if between 129mmol/L to 130 mmol/L or below) or if clinical signs of hypoglycaemia persist, especially in a shocked or comatose child. ^{1,5} Other IV fluids (eg Ringer lactate or halogen tube containing 5% dextrose 5% NaCl in equal proportions) are used to complete the resuscitation volume post starting normal saline. No entitlement to oral rehydration solution. Once clinically stabilised in PRU (pooled resuscitation unit), either on the way to/maintained on ward in the hospital prior to surgery or if a delay in theatre, normal saline and the child's management can be completed by medical and nursing staff.
The child's acquired immunocompromised status (HIV-exposed, PCR pending) emphasises an urgent surgical assessment is required ('if in doubt refer to theatre') but not the need for extensive HIV investigation with a PCR or lymphocyte count before surgery, which has no impact on urgency (the operating surgeon is fully empowered to take this decision). Once the infant has recovered from the acute illness, further HIV testing and, in cases of HIV reversal of PCR will include a lymphocyte count and CD4% ≤ 25% at 6-8 weeks of age, with start of ART alongside or shortly (up to 10 days later) after HIV diagnosis.6

Example management note would be: 'Fever and signs of peritonitis in a HIV exposed 11 month old with free fluid and possible free air after 48 hours symptoms. Stabilised with IV 100ml/kg 0.9% normal saline in the first hour and 10ml/kg 10% glucose IV for hypoglycaemia when serum glucose fell to 3.4mmol/L. Infant stabilised on normal saline – referred immediately to the theatre in the presence of continuous abdominal guarding and severe systemic sepsis signs under urgent surgical referral label (e.g dial the surgeon 0202891111)/.Gastrointestinal surgical team to arrange theatre listing. No need for further pre-operative HIV investigation; PCR and immunological staging of this infant to await post-resuscitation clinical improvement post expense of bowel resection or repair, till 6-8 weeks of age (as in other stabilised HIV exposed infants presenting acutely unwell). We will discuss HIV testing and start ART on recovery of child postoperatively if HIV infected'.

(Note - Muhimbili has 24/7 paediatric surgical services in the adjacent Kongowei Surgical Block. subsidiarily, the Prima Jania and or Mnazi Mmoja District hospitals in the Dar es Salaam metropolis have a 24 hour on call surgical team who will arrange transfer from Muhimbili ED rather than attempt to treat the child on their own facilities - indeed attempted intussusception reduction has a much worse outcome in setting of free air or signs of peritonitis >24 hours in the Tanzania setting ^{1}Both hospital early contact directly enhances and does not delay access to definitive surgical care).

In non-urban hospitals which cannot arrange immediate transfer within two hours to a hospital with surgical services (max 12 hours recommended by WHO/UNICEF in anybody presenting with peritonitis and whose abdominal Xray or US shows free air), successful non-operativerál 'watch and wait' management of non-ischaemic intussusception in uncomplicated cases lasting <24 hours has been reported following initial stabilisation in a PRU with IV fluids but this would not be appropriate in Dar es Salaam where availability of surgical services makes timely emergency surgery the safe option. ^{2,4}
The Royal College of Paediatrics and Child Health (RCPCH) and the British HIV Association jointly advise in the (2018) ublic recommendations that HIV infection is not indicated pending a negative HIV PCR result obtained at or after 4 weeks of age and that immediate surgery should not be delayed following this (and given a negative PCR) for HIV in-vestigation in a child with perforated or ischaemic intussusception. ^{7} Thus the guidelines from both Tanzania RAMIRRO (2017) and UK RCPCH (2018) agree in advising immediate surgical referral without awaiting HIV PCR results, especially when free air is present or peritonitis is present at more than 24 hours duration. ^{2,7}
In Muhimbili ED an example of less-than optimal management would be to await HIV PCR pending for 1-2 working days (such a practice now contradicts both the 2017 RAMIRRO guidelines and UK RCPCH (2018) recommendations for infants of unknown HIV status presenting with peritonitis and free air on imaging): such an approach puts this patient at high risk of death or severe long-term morbidity because of delay diagnostic to treatment time. Resuscitation with IV fluids in PRU and urgent surgical referral and management is the paramount priority in HIV-exposed untested infant presenting with signs of bowel obstruction and peritonitis lasting >24 hours and/or with free air detectable on imaging.
Paediatric ED doctors at Muhimbili have specific guidance on immediate action to take and a Memorandum of Understanding between ED and the Kongowei Surgical unit (signed by both department heads) agrees the decision to refer any child with abdominal guarding, systemic signs of peritonitis and/or free air seen on any imaging to theatre without need for any pre-operative stabilisation or attempts to 'stabilise' in PRU by medical means prior to transfer to theatre. If uncontactable the Porters or Theatre Sister can expedite the senior surgical staff being contacted by an automated reciprocal bleep.

