The classic triad of symptoms for intussusception is abdominal pain (usually colicky) > 2-5 hours in duration, a palpable abdominal mass (in up to 90%) and intermittent, projective (onset of blood or mucus-containing) non-bilious vomiting (in about 70%; bilious vomiting occurs later, especially in bowel hours 4-6, in about 90%), with irritability being a common accompaniment in children under 3 months or in those in whom a mass is not palpable.4 The whole Disney/Pixie and Duke1-3 classic and less common triad criteria (pain, vomiting, mass or palpable sausage/ 'intestinal obstruction' sign on abdominal examination) are fulfilled in about 90% of cases. In 10-15% of cases, a blood mixed non-bilious vomit is the only symptom.10 In adults, intussusception is often atypical (45% absent palpable mass and/or absent selective triad symptom(s)).
In well-appearing children typical of intussusception > 48 hours from onset, or in children who are unwell (febrile >38°C, lethargic, visible per rectal bleeding, or any shock), or if there is bilious vomiting, a swift referral for urgent hydrostatic reduction under ultrasound guidance (see below) is recommended by national guidelines (e.g British Society of Paediatric and Adolescent Gastroenterology (BSPAGH) and the Royal College of Paediatrics and Child Health (RCPCH)) and is indicated without the need for further imaging (e.g ultrasound) provided clinical suspicion remains high.5,11 In atypical cases (75% in adults), or in unwell or degenerating patients (those with evidence of a significant mucosal tear as suggested by clots, high stool frequency, blood loss >50ml/kg in 24 hours, abdominalgia or significant abdominal guarding), and in those who fail hydrostatic reduction or have a failed initial hydrostatic attempt (recurrent intussusception), or in cases up to 72 hours from symptom onset in which there is a minor and persistent/improving mass with a selective triad symptom (usually pain and/or vomiting) but no severe systemic symptoms (the non-emergency group), ultrasound confirmation is recommended before surgery (see below).

Diagnostic ultrasound examination is the first imaging choice, widely available (99% UK centres <30 minutes away), rapid (10-30 minutes), cheap, non-invasive and easy to perform with high sensitivity (85-95%) and specificity (95-100%) for the detection of intussusception.12-15 Signs include the 'target' or 'sausage' sign of concentric echogenic (hyperechoic) and echolucents (hypoechic) layers, extended appendix or inverted intussusception 'oral', 'cobblestone' or 'chicken wire' submucosal pattern. Ultrasound assessment should be performed by an experienced operator within a purpose-designed ultrasound service in 80-90% of cases by a paediatric radiologist (levels 1 and 2 services) in hospitals with >20,000 children living within a < 10 mile radius, or by a clinician with experience in ultrasound within an ultrasound non-designated paediatric service area (usually general paediatric areas). Ultrasound should be repeated if unsuccessful in an experienced hand within a median of 1.8 hours from the initial request, in order to facilitate early reduction in an otherwise well patient before admission.10, 13
Hydrostatic (reduction) tube enema under ultrasound guidance with normal saline or water, following failed ultrasound or after reduction and recurrence in up to 70% of such cases within 24 hours, reduces the need for surgery to <3%.16 The success rate is in the region of 80-90% for the first attempt in non-degenerate patients and should be performed under sedation if the acutely distressed child (most >6 months or with intractable pain) cannot be skilled or assumes it.17 Failure of the first (or subsequent) enema attempt or signs of an impending perforation (indicated by significant end colonic clots or improving/ persistent/decreasing frequency of stools) are a clear indication for urgent surgery, which has a minimal short bowel primary complication rate of 5% (<2% for ileocaecal intussusception).18 Patients with intussusception that reduces readily on enema under ultrasound will not require immediate readmission for attempted reduction in the event of recurrence within the first week.19,20
Surgery (resection of the intussusceptum) is indicated in cases of:
* Increasing abdominal pain and tender abdomen with feeding or other manoeuvres before or during attempted enema reduction and/or failed enema attempt (and/or appearance of the submucosal vascular pattern in the terminal bowel in the 'other segment': and significant end colonic clots indicated by blood-loss of >50ml/kg within previous 24 hours);
* Presence of an incomplete or failed reduction with signs of an impending perforation and/or a complete (lock) or near complete lock of the colon (ie longer than 4-5 'segments' of ischaemia/ dusky colour change of bowel wall in the terminal ileum/ right colon on ultrasound prior to attempted enema) on enema ultrasound;
* Clinical signs of bowel obstruction (e.g failure to pass flatus for >24 hours post first enema reduction, or impaction on attempted enema) after initial successful reduction;
* In patients who have failed reduction despite repeated enema attempts21 (usually >2 in this context);
* Adults with a long-standing painful mass exceeding 10cm in maximal diameter and/or >4cm diameter on plain X-ray in whom the history exceeds 48 hours, and where hydrostatic reduction is notpursued due to the likelihood of ischaemia of the intussusceptum.
Examples where urgent (within a few hours) surgical referral should be considered include failure of initial enema (e.g 4 patients out of 174, see Refs 5 and 18), recurrence and failure of attempted enema within an hour (including any with resultant new symptoms), significant delay to the first enema attempt >72 hours after onset of symptoms (in the majority), unwell patients and/or significant or deteriorating abdominal signs. The regional or local paediatric surgical service should be urgently contacted and definitive surgical management undertaken in these children/cases within 2-4 hours of a decision to operate.5

Reduction enema under ultrasound guidance is not recommended and surgery is indicated in the following high risk situations:


• Patients unwell at the time of initial assessment (e.g fever >38°C, lethargy, abdominal tenderness or per rectal bleeding or any shock);
• Trace/diffuse signs of free intraperitoneal fluid;23
• Duration of symptoms >72 hours especially in adults and in patients with any bilious vomiting;
• Clinical signs suggestive of mucosal rope sign or evidence (visible) of per rectal blood of >50ml and other segment colonic signs ('string sign', dot sign etc) presented with increasing abdomen pain before reduction;24
• Suspected enterocolitis as evidenced by high frequency (e.g more than 3 diarrheal stools/day) combined with clots/rectal blood of any volume, <48h in an otherwise unwell well appearing child invited for emergency reduction in the group defined by irritability or fever <38° with a mild, non selectenge visible per rectal bleeding.

In centres without experienced paediatric ultrasound expertise available within the time available (30 minutes in <90% of UK centres), or less-than 2 hours for adults with atypical features additionally meeting high risk criteria (see above), a diagnostic abdominal and/or pelvic CT or ultrasound with contrast in the forms of pneumatography or bowel thickening (only if plain features areconstitutive) should be obtained before attempted enema, under local guidance.
Plain abdominal or pelvic X-ray may be used as an first-line facility investigation before transfer diagnostic to the already referred well appearing child with typical features <48 hours and who is chosen to be managed at home with regular follow up after having a repeat ultrasound for non-diagnostic result within 24h of symptom onset (Fig1 in Ref 5).

The differential diagnoses in an otherwise healthy adolescent or young adult with acute bilateral parotid swelling without fever and with normal or augmented salivary flow (in the absence of pain or utilisation or sialoystia - as here with normal or augmented salivary flow from the salivary ducts) are (in order of probability):
- Recurrent parotitis (bilateral idiopathic parotitis), almost invariably occurring at week 2-12 of a menstrual cycle (see below and Observation) in women of reproductive age (in ~80-90% of cases): in recurrent parotitis the parotid glands are diffusely, red and soft swollen. ^{1}
- Viral parotitis (mumps-like parotitis after which the sialadenitis is mild, brief and punctate or diffuse but without autoimmune interstitial changes and with normal or augmented salivary flow from the salivary ducts ^{2} ). Mumps virus test in a case occurring out of the mumps season (April to October in Switzerland, the two epidemics in the two other seasons are much smaller ^{3} ) is likely to be negative.
- Stones in the Stensen's duct or gland are rare in young women (<5%) ^{4} and those causing acute parotitis presented as unilaterally or focal bilaterally painful swelling, or palpable sialolithiasis within the gland, with diminished or arrested salivary flow. The normal salivary excretion from the ducts in this case makes stone disease and/or gland obstructive disease unlikely.
Mumps virus infection, distal (such as coxsackie B and echovirus), or herpes zoster virus infection (rare without painful, then crusty facial skin lesions) and bacterial sialadenitis (Streptococcus or Staphylocococcus) are rare (<10%). The absence of fever, exertional and preferably at rest salivary flow normal or augmented from the ducts and soft gland swelling put these diagnoses in the lower order of probability in this case.
More rare causes such as transient ductal obstruction by a benign tumour or alveolar abscess of the buccal minor salivary glands, sarcoidosis, and other systemic or granulomatous diseases, autoimmune interstitial parotitis and Sjögren's syndrome are even less likely in this demographic and in this clinical presentation (normal salivary flow and soft gland swelling). Post-operative parotitis is also excluded by the anamnestic information.
There is no indication for acoustic sialography, sampling from the parotid gland or serological tests (e.g. for parotid stone, sarcoidosis, autoimmune interstitial parotitis and Sjögren syndrome). ^{5}
No treatment is recommended from the start other than reassurance about the benign nature of recurrent parotid inflammation ^{1} and advice to come for observation during the next menstrual cycle if symptoms recur. This represents the policy for diagnosis and management consistently recommended by SSO (Société Suisse d'ORL et de Cynaciologie), ESA, SGO/SSCH and SGOR (the Swiss Societies of Otorhinolaryngology, Neuroradiology and of Gynaecology and Obstetrics/Society of Swiss Clinical Hormonology and of ORL) and Swiss Health Insurance. ^{6}
The patient should be advised to come to hospital if she develops any of the following:
- fever;
- painful, fluctuant swelling (>1.5cm in diameter) of one gland;
- persisting or augmented pain despite normal salivary flow;
- diminished or arrested salivary flow from one gland.
The patient returned after a week in the second week of her new menstrual cycle with the same symptoms as initially. There was once again no fever and normal salivary excretion from the salivary ducts and repeated examination showed diffuse red and soft swelling of the parotid glands. No other complaint or abnormal examination. In the absence of fever or localising signs (e.g osteitis of the mandible or preauricular swelling), no specific investigations should be undertaken and she should be reassured and sent home. ^{1,5,6} She can then be followed and evaluated for symptoms about 1-2 weeks before the next expected menstrual cycle, if symptoms recur.
Example referral letter to the patient regarding her initial consultation and subsequent follow-up visit would be: 'Your recurrence of swelling in both parotid glands in the next menstrual cycle has been brought to your attention. Most recurrent parotitis resolves spontaneously and without the need for any examination and/or any treatment during the symptoms. These recurrences of non painful, soft and diffuse parotid gland swelling, without fever or diminished salivary flow (normal or augmented salivary flow from the salivary ducts) usually occur 2-12 days after the start of the menstrual period. For this reason, we would like you to follow this non painful swelling (which should come and go with the symptoms of cyclical recurrent parotitis without the need for reseeing you whilst the swelling is present). If you notice any change: painful and/or fluctuant swelling of one gland, fever or diminished or arrested salivary flow from one gland, then please see a doctor (i.e. again contact our department EH 150 in this hospital) but only for these abnormal symptoms. Please come for reevaluation 1-2 weeks before your next period if the swelling persists at that time'.
In contrast, a prompt investigation and specific treatment is not recommended in the acute phase of a painful or fluctuant swelling of a parotid gland alone, of uniformly diminished salivary flow from one parotid gland, or if there is fever, or in case of swollen and painful, less than 1.5 cm in diameter lump within a gland which then resolved by the next day. The ENT specialist should however see the patient, with evaluation including acoustic sialography and/or overnight low volume parotid aspiration if these symptoms persist for instance 1-2 weeks premenstrually, or indeed at any time if the clinical examination is indicative of 'bilateral or unilateral localised parotid swelling with utilisation' which may be suggestive of adenolith or very rarely of autoimmune interstitial parotitis/Sjögren syndrome. ^{1,5,6} In this case, an elimination examination including additional blood tests (autoantibodies ANA, RF, anti-SSA and anti-SSB, IgG and IgA to Sjögren's Syndrome-related anti-nucleolar ribonucleoprrotein (anti-SSB-RNP) and IgG to methyl-lgG (anti-ICA) as well as of amylase and immunoglobulins) and in some cases an ultrasound or parotid sialography might then be undertaken. <3% of patients, mostly zero, who present with recurrent parotid gland inflammation have ISS in the presence of a normal flow. ^{7}
Example prescription for the patient: 'Please follow this non-painful swelling which should come and go with your menstrual cycle, without examining here whilst the swelling is present. If this swelling comes back before you see your next menstuation or if you develop any of the following: fever, painful and/or fluctuant swelling of one conjointly palpable (larger than 1.5cm) parotid gland, decreased or arrested salivary flow from one gland, come to hospital for consultation in the Emergency (number of the Emergency office EH 150 in this hospital).'
Translated from the corresponding recommendation by the SSO.(1) 'Patient TRIHAND from 'recurring reversible parotid (periodic bilateral diffuse gland swelling also known as recurrent parotitis) with normal or augmented salivary flow from the salivary duct must be (sent home and) seen again when worse' in order words only if clinical indication of painful swelling larger or equal to 1.5 cm, fever, abnormal sialo-olfactory flow.'

The Swiss Society of Pneumology (SSP/ SSWK) and the Swiss Society of Paediatrics (SSP/ SGPCH) recommend in the presence of viral infection continuing, without change, the daily inhaled corticosteroids (≥200 micrograms beclomethasone dipropionate or equivalent per day ^{1} ), as well as self administration of a short-acting beta _{2} - agonist (SABA) on demand for relief of asthma symptoms (it is not considered necessary to increase the dose of ICS or to add a preventer dose at night for 2-3 days). The SSP/ SSWK and SSP/ SGPCH discourage the SABA 'peak flow chart', frequent home monitoring of peak expiratory flow (PEF) or regular visits in practice during viral infections (unless there is progressive increase in symptoms and/or nocturnal symptoms or worsening of lung function to <70-80% of pre-infection personal best PEF (BBPEF): in such cases an increase of ICS to budesonide 400 micrograms (or equivalent) daily in combination with SABA as needed should be considered). Written information explaining that asthma symptoms are often exacerbated during viral infections but that no change to treatment is generally needed and giving instructions on treatment with SABA and use of the medication should be provided to the parents. ^{1}
Therefore, reassurance of the mother alone following an explanation of this approach with written information for the parents usually suffices. A consultation in the practice or even a postponed consultation 7-10 days later is not required unless there is an increase of symptoms as mentioned above. ^{1} The child does not need to be seen in the hospital or the paediatric emergency service during viral upper respiratory tract infection.
Example of the printed information to be provided to the parents:
'The current viral infection (e.g. rhinopharyngitis) will also have an effect on your child's asthma. She may wheeze more during this time and run more quickly out of breath when playing. This does not mean that the asthma is getting worse, or that she needs her preventer inhaler more frequently. Her preventer tablets only work if she uses them regularly every day and at the right time (e.g. in the morning before breakfast). Please give her her preventive inhaler daily as usual and use the reliever (SABA) inhaler for immediate relief of asthma symptoms (coughing and/or wheezing and/or rapid breathing which gets worse). When her symptoms improve, the SABA use will generally decrease. The reliever inhaler can be used as often as needed (e.g. every 1 to 4 hours). Please inform our practice prior to an unscheduled visit in the (paediatric) emergency service or office consultation only if your child has an increase of symptoms to a point where lung function falls below 70-80% of BBPEF, or if there are nocturnal symptoms'. ^{1}

A revision of the asthma management plan (in accordance with the SSA/ SSw - guideline 'Asthma in children and adolescents', ^{2} last updated in 2019) including parents' understanding of the expected increase in SABA use in infectious periods should already have taken place during the child's at least two routine office visits in the preceding six months. ^{1}
For this patient, no appointment or consultation in the practice is therefore required today. The mother can be reassured: 'If she develops symptoms such as coughing and/or difficulty in catching breath that last more than 2 successive days, doesn't improve with more frequent SABA use or gets up at night wheezing then we need to see you again within the next 1-3 workdays.' ^{1}
Example of what the paediatrician can say to the mother in a consultation lasting around 5 minutes is: 'Her viral infection will increase her breathlessness and wheezing over the next few days but this does not mean that her asthma is getting worse. It will be the same as it was during the rhinovirus infection in autumn/winter last year - please just continue to give her the preventer as usual and only use the reliever (SABA) as much as she needs for the wheezing and breathlessness. We will see you at her next routine visit (in [..] weeks). If she gets worse between then and then please contact us beforehand.' ^{1}

Neste caso é adequado suspender o tratamento com hormônios Disney-T4 de reposição (e mesmowormênio se em uso) e aguardar pelo menos 6-8 semanas, durante as quais deve haver acompanhamento ambulatorial trimestral ou bimestral, antes de realizar nova cintilografia com pertechnetato (99mTcO4-). ^{1, 2}
Exemções às regras de investigação etiológica não são necessárias em caso de glândula tireoide eutrófica, com volume, parênquima e fluxo sanguíneo normais na ultrassonografia ou em crianças com captação de 0,1 a 3,0% em cintilografia e ausência de sinais clínicos ou laboratoriais de hipotireoidismo. ^{1, 2}  É geralmente incurável captação de até 4% e em alguns casos até 5-5,9%, o que é o caso nessa paciente. ^{2}
Exemplos de situações em que procede a indicação de outras investigações (como escovanemento de fígado, stool, DNA no fezes, virtoscopia multifocale, mutação sequenciamento (p.ex. TSHR, NDUFB9, IGSF1, MYCN entre outros): glândula tiresoide hipotrófica (volume ≤ 50% do predito ou parênquima ausente e/ou paredes tireoide delgadas), captação de 0% e/ou presença de sinais e sintomas clínicos de hipotireoidismo não reversível após suspensão de reposição. ^{1,2}
Em objetivo claro, não é necessária reinauguração do tratamento hormonal da paciente e a ultrassonografia e a próxima cintilografia (com realização depois de 6-8 semanas de espera) devem ser aguardadas.

Observações da Carta de Diretriz do Consenso Brasileiro de Hipo e Hipertireoidismo Congênitos da Sociedade Brasileira de Endocrinologia e Metabologia (SBEM) 2017: ^{1} "Pacientes ao longo do crescimento que demonstram glândula tireoide com volume, parênquima e fluxo sanguíneo normais, sem sinais clínicos ou laboratoriais de hipotireoidismo e captação de até 3% em cintilografia podem ter a suspensão do tratamento Bobes, aguardando, no mínimo, 6-8 semanas e avaliação reavaliando a captação. Persistindo captação baixa (0,1 a 3%), é recomendável encerrar a investigação e permanecer sem reposição, por evitarem riscos associados ao tratamento desnecessário, tais como crescimento excessivo, entre outros."

Exemplo de Atlas deSaída/do Ambulatório de Hipotireoidismo Congênito e Materno fetal: "Paciente suspensa de Reposição de T4, que aguardará nova cintilografia em 2 meses (em 10, 15 ou 30 de [mês actual]), pois eutiroide na UST acoplada a baixa captação de litio (0,1% a 4%) sem sinais clínicos"
Exemplo de Carta médica: "Segue anexa carta de aviso de suspensão do tratamento hormonal com T4 associado ou não à T3, e resumo das orientações providas no momento da consulta (anexo). A paciente e/ou cuidador será reavaliado no ambulatório em [data e mês, comprovando pelo menos 6-8 semanas de suspensão de reposição]. Desta vez será solicitada a realização de nova cintilografia com 99mTcO4- devido a captação de 0,1%…".
